// 2020 GS1 (Prelims) Set A — Transcribed from "E:\UPSC question paper\2020\2020.docx"
// Contains question, choices (A, B, C, D), answer, explanation, and upsc review points
export const paper = { year: 2020, name: 'GS1', set: 'A' };

export const questions = [
{"no":1,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding Aadhaar:\nAadhaar metadata cannot be stored for more than three months.\nState cannot enter into any contract with private corporations for sharing of Aadhaar data.\nAadhaar is mandatory for obtaining insurance products.\nAadhaar is mandatory for getting benefits funded out of the Consolidated Fund of India.","A":"1 and 4 only","B":"2 and 4 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3 only","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Fundamental Rights","tags":["Aadhaar","Right to Privacy","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"The source identifies statements 2 and 4 as correct.\nAadhaar-related provisions distinguish between permitted use/sharing of information and mandatory authentication for specified benefits.\nAadhaar is not universally mandatory for obtaining insurance products.\nThe source therefore selects 2 and 4.","revisionPoints":"Aadhaar is a unique identity system.\nAuthentication and identification are distinct concepts.\nAadhaar requirements depend on the specific service or statutory provision.\nAvoid assuming Aadhaar is mandatory for every financial or government service."},
{"no":2,"text":"Rajya Sabha has equal powers with Lok Sabha in:","A":"The matter of creating new All India Services","B":"Amending the Constitution","C":"The removal of the Government","D":"Making cut motions","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Rajya Sabha","Lok Sabha","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Constitutional amendment bills require approval by both Houses of Parliament.\nNeither House has an ordinary unilateral power to amend the Constitution.\nIn contrast, the Lok Sabha has greater powers in matters such as removal of the Council of Ministers and financial procedures.\nThe source answer is B.","revisionPoints":"Constitutional amendment → generally requires both Houses.\nMoney Bills → Lok Sabha has greater authority.\nCouncil of Ministers → collectively responsible to Lok Sabha.\nRajya Sabha has special powers in certain federal matters."},
{"no":3,"text":"With reference to funds under the Members of Parliament Local Area Development Scheme (MPLADS), which statements are correct?\nMPLADS funds must be used to create durable assets like physical infrastructure for health, education, etc.\nA specified portion of each MP's fund must benefit SC/ST populations.\nMPLADS funds are sanctioned yearly and unused funds cannot be carried forward.\nThe district authority must inspect at least 10% of all works under implementation every year.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 and 4 only","C":"1, 2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 4 only","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["MPLADS","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"MPLADS is intended for creation of durable community assets.\nA specified share is earmarked for works benefiting SC/ST populations.\nUnspent funds can be carried forward, so statement 3 is incorrect.\nThe source identifies inspection of at least 10% of works as a requirement.\nHence, 1, 2 and 4 are correct.","revisionPoints":"MPLADS → constituency-level development works.\nFocus → durable community assets.\nSC/ST benefit → earmarked component.\nDistrict authority → implementation and monitoring role.\nUnutilized funds are not simply treated as permanently lapsing."},
{"no":4,"text":"Which category of Fundamental Rights incorporates protection against untouchability as a form of discrimination?","A":"Right against Exploitation","B":"Right to Freedom","C":"Right to Constitutional Remedies","D":"Right to Equality","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Fundamental Rights","tags":["Right to Equality","Untouchability","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Article 17 abolishes untouchability.\nArticle 17 forms part of the Right to Equality under Part III.\nIt prohibits the practice of untouchability in any form.\nTherefore, the correct answer is Right to Equality.","revisionPoints":"Article 14 → Equality before law.\nArticle 15 → Prohibition of discrimination.\nArticle 16 → Equality of opportunity in public employment.\nArticle 17 → Abolition of untouchability.\nArticle 18 → Abolition of titles."},
{"no":5,"text":"In India, separation of judiciary from the executive is enjoined by:","A":"Preamble","B":"Directive Principle of State Policy","C":"Seventh Schedule","D":"Conventional practice","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["DPSP","Separation of Powers","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Article 50 directs the State to take steps to separate the judiciary from the executive.\nArticle 50 is located in Part IV, dealing with Directive Principles of State Policy.\nTherefore, it is a constitutional directive rather than merely a convention.","revisionPoints":"Article 50 → separation of judiciary from executive.\nPart IV → Directive Principles.\nDPSPs are generally not enforceable by courts.\nThey guide the State in policy and law-making."},
{"no":6,"text":"Along with the Budget, the Finance Minister places the Macro Economic Framework Statement before Parliament because it is mandated by:","A":"Long-standing parliamentary convention","B":"Articles 112 and 110(1)","C":"Article 113","D":"Fiscal Responsibility and Budget Management Act, 2003","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["FRBM Act","Union Budget","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"The Macro Economic Framework Statement is one of the fiscal policy documents presented along with the Budget.\nIts presentation is linked to requirements under the FRBM Act, 2003.\nTherefore, the source identifies option D.","revisionPoints":"FRBM → fiscal discipline framework.\nBudget documents include multiple statements beyond the Annual Financial Statement.\nMacro Economic Framework → broader economic and fiscal outlook.\nAnnual Financial Statement → constitutionally linked to Article 112."},
{"no":7,"text":"A constitutional government is by definition a:","A":"Government by legislature","B":"Popular government","C":"Multi-party government","D":"Limited government","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Constitutionalism","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Constitutional government means governmental power is limited and regulated by constitutional rules.\nThe Constitution establishes limits on governmental authority.\nTherefore, constitutionalism is closely associated with limited government.","revisionPoints":"Constitutionalism → limitation of governmental power.\nConstitution → framework for exercise of state authority.\nRule of law → government acts according to law.\nConstitutional government need not necessarily be multi-party."},
{"no":8,"text":"Other than Fundamental Rights, which parts of the Constitution reflect principles of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights (1948)?\nPreamble\nDirective Principles of State Policy\nFundamental Duties","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["UDHR","Fundamental Rights","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"The source identifies all three as reflecting principles associated with the UDHR.\nThe Preamble contains ideals such as dignity, equality and justice.\nDPSPs promote social and economic welfare.\nFundamental Duties include responsibilities connected with respect for national and social values.","revisionPoints":"UDHR adopted in 1948.\nFundamental Rights → individual rights.\nDPSPs → social and economic objectives.\nFundamental Duties → constitutional responsibilities of citizens.\nPreamble → foundational constitutional ideals."},
{"no":9,"text":"In India, Legal Services Authorities provide free legal services to which types of citizens?\nPerson with annual income below ₹1,00,000\nTransgender person with annual income below ₹2,00,000\nOBC member with annual income below ₹3,00,000\nAll senior citizens","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 and 4 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1 and 4 only","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["Legal Services Authorities","Free Legal Aid","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"The source answer is 1 and 2 only.\nLegal Services Authorities provide free legal assistance to specified categories of persons under the legal services framework.\nIncome limits may be prescribed for certain categories.\nMerely being an OBC member or senior citizen does not automatically establish eligibility under the statements given.","revisionPoints":"Legal aid → promotes access to justice.\nLegal Services Authorities operate under the legal services framework.\nEligibility depends on specified categories and conditions.\nFree legal services are connected with the constitutional objective of equal access to justice."},
{"no":10,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nInternational agreement/set-up\nSubject\n1. Alma-Ata Declaration\nHealthcare of the people\n2. Hague Convention\nBiological and chemical weapons\n3. Talanoa Dialogue\nGlobal climate change\n4. Under2 Coalition\nChild rights","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"4 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"2, 3 and 4 only","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Organizations","tags":["Alma-Ata Declaration","Talanoa Dialogue","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Alma-Ata Declaration → primary health care.\nTalanoa Dialogue → climate-change action and discussion.\nThe Hague Convention is not correctly matched with biological and chemical weapons in this context.\nUnder2 Coalition is associated with climate action by subnational governments, not child rights.","revisionPoints":"Alma-Ata → Primary Health Care.\nTalanoa Dialogue → climate change.\nUnder2 Coalition → subnational climate action.\nHague Conventions → several international conventions covering different legal/humanitarian subjects."},
{"no":11,"text":"A Parliamentary System of Government is one in which:","A":"All political parties in Parliament are represented in Government","B":"Government is responsible to Parliament and can be removed by it","C":"Government is elected by people and can be removed by them","D":"Government is chosen by Parliament but cannot be removed before fixed term","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Parliamentary System","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"In a parliamentary system, the executive is responsible to the legislature.\nIn India, the Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha.\nLoss of majority can result in the government having to resign.\nHence, option B is correct.","revisionPoints":"Parliamentary system → executive-legislature relationship.\nCouncil of Ministers → collectively responsible to Lok Sabha.\nPrime Minister → head of the real executive.\nPresident → constitutional/formal head at Union level.\nCollective responsibility is a key feature."},
{"no":12,"text":"Which part of the Constitution of India declares the ideal of a Welfare State?","A":"Directive Principles of State Policy","B":"Fundamental Rights","C":"Preamble","D":"Seventh Schedule","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["DPSP","Welfare State","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"DPSPs provide social and economic objectives for the State.\nThey aim at creating conditions associated with a welfare-oriented state.\nThey guide governments in policy-making and legislation.\nTherefore, the answer is DPSP.","revisionPoints":"DPSPs → Part IV.\nArticle 37 → DPSPs are not enforceable by courts.\nThey are fundamental in governance.\nWelfare-state objectives are strongly reflected in DPSPs."},
{"no":13,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe Constitution of India defines its 'basic structure' in terms of federalism, secularism, fundamental rights and democracy.\nThe Constitution of India provides for 'judicial review' to safeguard citizens' liberties and preserve the ideals on which the Constitution is based.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Basic Structure Doctrine","Judicial Review","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"The Constitution does not itself define the expression \"basic structure.\"\nThe basic structure doctrine developed through Supreme Court jurisprudence.\nAlthough judicial review is an important constitutional principle, the statement's formulation does not represent an explicit constitutional declaration in the manner stated.\nThe source answer is D.","revisionPoints":"Basic Structure Doctrine → judicially evolved.\nKesavananda Bharati case (1973) → central to the doctrine.\nParliament's amending power is not unlimited.\nJudicial review → courts examine constitutional validity of laws/actions."},
{"no":14,"text":"One common agreement between Gandhism and Marxism is:","A":"The final goal of a stateless society","B":"Class struggle","C":"Abolition of private property","D":"Economic determinism","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Gandhism","Marxism","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"The source identifies the stateless society as the common element.\nMarxism envisages the eventual withering away of the state in a classless society.\nGandhian thought also envisages a highly decentralized social order with minimal coercive authority.\nThe other options do not represent a common feature of both ideologies.","revisionPoints":"Marxism → class struggle and material/economic analysis.\nGandhism → non-violence, decentralization and ethical politics.\nMarxism traditionally seeks abolition of class divisions.\nGandhian thought emphasizes social harmony rather than class conflict."},
{"no":15,"text":"In the context of India, which is the characteristic appropriate for bureaucracy?","A":"Agency for widening parliamentary democracy","B":"Agency for strengthening federalism","C":"Agency for facilitating political stability and economic growth","D":"Agency for implementation of public policy","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Public Administration","tags":["Bureaucracy","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Bureaucracy forms the permanent administrative machinery of government.\nIt translates government decisions and policies into administrative action.\nElected governments formulate broad policy directions, while the bureaucracy plays a major role in implementation.\nHence, option D is correct.","revisionPoints":"Bureaucracy → permanent executive.\nPolitical executive → elected and temporary.\nPermanent executive → continuity in administration.\nMajor function → policy implementation and administration.\nBureaucracy operates within legal and constitutional frameworks."},
{"no":16,"text":"The Preamble to the Constitution of India is:","A":"A part of Constitution but has no legal effect","B":"Not a part of Constitution and has no legal effect","C":"A part of Constitution and has same legal effect as any other part","D":"A part of Constitution but has no legal effect independently of other parts","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Preamble","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"The Preamble is considered part of the Constitution.\nHowever, it does not operate as an independent source of substantive power.\nIt helps in understanding the objectives and philosophy of the Constitution.\nThe source answer is D.","revisionPoints":"Kesavananda Bharati (1973) → Preamble recognized as part of Constitution.\nPreamble contains constitutional objectives and ideals.\nPreamble can assist constitutional interpretation.\nIt does not independently confer enforceable powers."},
{"no":17,"text":"'Gold Tranche' or Reserve Tranche refers to:","A":"Loan system of the World Bank","B":"One of the operations of a Central Bank","C":"Credit system granted by WTO","D":"Credit system granted by IMF to its members","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Financial Institutions","tags":["IMF","Gold Tranche","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"The Reserve Tranche Position is associated with a country's position within the IMF.\nIt represents a portion of a member country's quota that can be accessed under specified conditions.\nTherefore, the source identifies the IMF as the relevant institution.","revisionPoints":"IMF → international monetary institution.\nMember countries have quotas in the IMF.\nReserve Tranche Position is connected with access to IMF resources.\nDo not confuse IMF facilities with World Bank development lending."},
{"no":18,"text":"With reference to Part IV of the Constitution of India, which statements are correct?\nThey shall be enforceable by courts.\nThey shall not be enforceable by any court.\nPrinciples laid down in this Part are to influence the making of laws by the State.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"2 and 3 only","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["DPSP","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Part IV contains the Directive Principles of State Policy.\nUnder Article 37, DPSPs are not enforceable by any court.\nHowever, they are fundamental in the governance of the country.\nThey guide the State in making laws and policies.","revisionPoints":"Part IV → DPSP.\nArticle 37 → non-justiciable.\nDPSPs → fundamental in governance.\nThey aim at social, economic and political justice.\nFundamental Rights and DPSPs are intended to complement each other."},
{"no":19,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nAccording to the Constitution, a person eligible to vote can be made a State Minister for six months even if not a member of the State Legislature.\nAccording to the Representation of the People Act, 1951, a person convicted and sentenced to imprisonment for five years is permanently disqualified from contesting elections even after release.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Article 164","Representation of the People Act","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"A non-member can become a minister for a maximum period of six months, but the constitutional requirement involves eligibility to become a member of the legislature, not simply eligibility to vote.\nUnder election law, disqualification following conviction is not automatically permanent after release merely because the sentence was five years.\nTherefore, both statements are incorrect as framed.","revisionPoints":"Article 164(4) → six-month rule for a non-legislator minister.\nWithin six months, the person must become a member of the State Legislature.\nVoting eligibility ≠ eligibility to become a legislator.\nElection disqualifications are governed by constitutional and statutory provisions."},
{"no":20,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe President of India can summon a session of Parliament at such place as he/she thinks fit.\nThe Constitution provides for three sessions of Parliament in a year, but it is not mandatory to conduct all three.\nThere is no minimum number of days that Parliament is required to meet in a year.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"2 and 3 only","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Parliament Sessions","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"The President summons and prorogues Parliament under the constitutional framework.\nThe Constitution does not prescribe three mandatory sessions every year.\nIt requires that the gap between two sessions should not exceed six months.\nThe Constitution also does not prescribe a minimum number of sitting days per year.\nTherefore, statements 1 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"President → summons Parliament.\nMaximum gap between two sessions → 6 months.\nConstitution does not mandate exactly three sessions annually.\nConstitution does not prescribe a fixed minimum number of sitting days.\nSessions include Budget, Monsoon and Winter sessions as parliamentary practice, not as a constitutional requirement.\nUPSC – Questions 21–40"},
{"no":21,"text":"With reference to the history of India, consider the following pairs:\nTerm\nDescription\n1. Aurang\nIn-charge of treasury of the State\n2. Banian\nIndian agent of the East India Company\n3. Mirasidar\nDesignated revenue payer to the State","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Medieval Terminology","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Banian refers to an Indian agent associated with the East India Company.\nMirasidar refers to a person holding hereditary rights in land and associated with revenue payment.\nThe description given for Aurang is incorrect.\nTherefore, pairs 2 and 3 are correctly matched.","revisionPoints":"Banian → Indian commercial/administrative intermediary.\nMirasidar → hereditary landholder/revenue-related term.\nHistorical administrative terminology can vary by period and region.\nMatch terms with their specific historical context."},
{"no":22,"text":"With reference to the religious history of India, consider the following statements:\nSthaviravadins belong to Mahayana Buddhism.\nLokottaravadin sect was an offshoot of Mahasanghika sect.\nDeification of Buddha by Mahasanghikas fostered Mahayana Buddhism.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Buddhism","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"The source identifies statements 2 and 3 as correct.\nLokottaravadins were associated with the Mahasanghika tradition.\nThe Mahasanghika tendency toward a more transcendent conception of Buddha is associated with the development of ideas that later became important in Mahayana.\nStatement 1 is incorrect because Sthaviravada is associated with the older Buddhist tradition, not Mahayana.","revisionPoints":"Buddhism developed several schools after the early Buddhist period.\nMahasanghika → one of the major early Buddhist schools.\nLokottaravada → associated with Mahasanghika.\nMahayana → greater emphasis on Bodhisattva ideal and transcendental Buddha concepts."},
{"no":23,"text":"Which statement correctly explains the impact of the Industrial Revolution on India during the first half of the nineteenth century?","A":"Indian handicrafts were ruined.","B":"Machines were introduced in Indian textile industry in large numbers.","C":"Railway lines were laid in many parts of India.","D":"Heavy duties were imposed on imports of British manufactures.","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Industrial Revolution","Deindustrialization","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"British machine-made goods increasingly entered the Indian market.\nIndian traditional handicrafts faced severe competition from industrially produced British textiles.\nThis contributed to the decline of several Indian handicraft industries.\nThe other options do not correctly describe the major impact during this period.","revisionPoints":"Industrial Revolution → mechanized mass production.\nBritish manufactured goods → increasing penetration of Indian markets.\nIndian handicrafts → faced competition and decline.\nColonial economic policies also contributed to deindustrialization."},
{"no":24,"text":"Arrange the following events in chronological order, starting from the earliest:\nRise of Pratiharas under King Bhoja\nEstablishment of Pallava power under Mahendravarman I\nEstablishment of Chola power by Parantaka I\nPala dynasty founded by Gopala","A":"2 – 1 – 4 – 3","B":"3 – 1 – 4 – 2","C":"2 – 4 – 1 – 3","D":"3 – 4 – 1 – 2","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Regional Kingdoms","Chronology","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Pallava power under Mahendravarman I came first.\nThe Pala dynasty was founded by Gopala subsequently.\nThe rise of the Pratihara ruler Bhoja followed.\nParantaka I established Chola power later.\nHence, the correct sequence is 2 → 4 → 1 → 3.","revisionPoints":"Mahendravarman I → Pallava ruler.\nGopala → founder of Pala dynasty.\nBhoja → major Pratihara ruler.\nParantaka I → important early Chola ruler.\nChronology questions require identifying approximate reign periods."},
{"no":25,"text":"Which phrase defines the nature of the Hundi generally referred to in sources of the post-Harsha period?","A":"Advisory issued by the king to subordinates","B":"Diary maintained for daily accounts","C":"A bill of exchange","D":"Order from a feudal lord to subordinates","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Hundi","Post-Harsha Period","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"A hundi was a financial instrument used in trade and credit transactions.\nIt functioned broadly like a bill of exchange.\nIt facilitated transfer of money and commercial transactions without physically moving cash.\nTherefore, option C is correct.","revisionPoints":"Hundi → traditional Indian credit/remittance instrument.\nUsed extensively by merchants.\nFacilitated long-distance trade.\nImportant in the history of Indian commercial institutions."},
{"no":26,"text":"With reference to Desher Katha, written by Sakharam Ganesh Deuskar, consider the following:\nIt warned against the Colonial State's hypnotic conquest of the mind.\nIt inspired Swadeshi street plays and folk songs.\nDeuskar used the term 'desh' specifically in the context of Bengal.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Desher Katha","Swadeshi Movement","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Desher Katha was an important nationalist work associated with the Swadeshi period.\nIt criticized the economic and psychological impact of colonial rule.\nIt contributed to nationalist cultural activities.\nThe source identifies statements 1 and 2 as correct.","revisionPoints":"Sakharam Ganesh Deuskar → nationalist writer.\nDesher Katha → critique of colonial economic exploitation.\nSwadeshi movement → boycott + indigenous production + nationalist mobilization.\nLiterature and popular culture played an important role in nationalist politics."},
{"no":27,"text":"The Gandhi-Irwin Pact included which of the following?\nInvitation to Congress to participate in the Round Table Conference.\nWithdrawal of ordinances promulgated in connection with the Civil Disobedience Movement.\nAcceptance of Gandhiji's suggestion for enquiry into police excesses.\nRelease of only those prisoners who were not charged with violence.","A":"1 only","B":"1, 2 and 4 only","C":"3 only","D":"2, 3 and 4 only","ans":null,"category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Gandhi-Irwin Pact","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"The source explicitly marks this question as “Dropped Question.”\nTherefore, no final answer should be treated as valid from this source.\nFor study purposes, remember that the Gandhi-Irwin Pact was associated with suspension of the Civil Disobedience Movement and Congress participation in the Second Round Table Conference.","revisionPoints":"Gandhi-Irwin Pact → 1931.\nAssociated with the Civil Disobedience Movement.\nGandhi participated in the Second Round Table Conference.\nPact preceded Gandhi's participation in the conference.\nUPSC dropped this question, so don't memorize an option as the official answer.","dropped":true},
{"no":28,"text":"The Vital-Vidhvansak, the first monthly journal to have untouchable people as its target audience, was published by:","A":"Gopal Baba Walangkar","B":"Jyotiba Phule","C":"M.K. Gandhi","D":"B.R. Ambedkar","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Anti-Untouchability Movement","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Gopal Baba Walangkar was an early activist associated with the movement against caste discrimination.\nHe published Vital-Vidhvansak.\nThe journal focused specifically on issues affecting oppressed/untouchable communities.\nHence, option A is correct.","revisionPoints":"Gopal Baba Walangkar → early anti-caste activist.\nVital-Vidhvansak → associated with Dalit social reform.\nJyotiba Phule → major anti-caste and social reformer.\nAmbedkar → later became a major constitutional and social rights leader."},
{"no":29,"text":"With reference to Indian history, the terms Kulyavapa and Dronavapa denote:","A":"Measurement of land","B":"Coins of different monetary value","C":"Classification of urban land","D":"Religious rituals","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Land Measurement","Ancient Terminology","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Kulyavapa and Dronavapa were units associated with measurement of land.\nThey occur in historical land-grant and administrative contexts.\nTherefore, option A is correct.","revisionPoints":"Ancient Indian inscriptions contain several land-measurement terms.\nUnits could vary by period and region.\nLand grants are important sources for reconstructing agrarian history.\nHistorical measurement terms are frequently tested through matching questions."},
{"no":30,"text":"Who among the following rulers advised his subjects through this inscription?\n“Whosoever praises his religious sect or blames other sects out of excessive devotion to his own sect... injures his own sect...”","A":"Ashoka","B":"Samudragupta","C":"Harshavardhana","D":"Krishnadeva Raya","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Ashoka","Edicts","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"The inscription reflects Ashoka's emphasis on religious tolerance and respect for different sects.\nAshoka encouraged restraint in criticizing other religious traditions.\nSuch messages form part of the ethical and social principles reflected in his edicts.\nTherefore, the answer is Ashoka.","revisionPoints":"Ashoka → Mauryan emperor.\nMajor inscriptions → important sources for Mauryan history.\nDhamma → ethical and social principles promoted by Ashoka.\nReligious tolerance → important theme in Ashokan inscriptions."},
{"no":31,"text":"With reference to the cultural history of India, which is the correct description of Paramitas?","A":"Earliest Dharmashastra texts written in aphoristic style","B":"Philosophical schools that rejected Vedic authority","C":"Perfections whose attainment led to the Bodhisattva path","D":"Powerful merchant guilds of early medieval South India","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Buddhism","Bodhisattva","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Paramitas are perfections cultivated by a Bodhisattva.\nThey represent qualities that must be developed on the path toward enlightenment.\nThey are especially important in Mahayana Buddhist thought.\nHence, option C is correct.","revisionPoints":"Paramita → “perfection” or completion of a virtue.\nAssociated strongly with the Bodhisattva ideal.\nExamples include generosity, morality and patience.\nMahayana → strong emphasis on Bodhisattva path."},
{"no":32,"text":"The Rakhmabai case of 1884 revolved around:\nWomen's right to gain education\nAge of consent\nRestitution of conjugal rights","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Rakhmabai Case","Age of Consent","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"The Rakhmabai case centered on marriage, consent and restitution of conjugal rights.\nIt became an important public debate concerning women's rights and marriage in colonial India.\nThe source identifies 2 and 3 as correct.","revisionPoints":"Rakhmabai → important figure in debates on women's rights.\nCase involved restitution of conjugal rights.\nIt contributed to wider debates over marriage and consent.\nWomen's education and reform became major themes of 19th-century social reform."},
{"no":33,"text":"Indigo cultivation in India declined by the beginning of the 20th century because of:","A":"Peasant resistance to oppressive conduct of planters","B":"Its unprofitability in world market because of new inventions","C":"National leaders' opposition","D":"Government control over planters","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Indigo Cultivation","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"The development of synthetic indigo reduced the international demand for natural indigo.\nThis made natural indigo cultivation increasingly unprofitable.\nPeasant resistance had earlier affected indigo cultivation, but the source identifies technological developments and synthetic dyes as the key reason for its decline by the beginning of the 20th century.","revisionPoints":"Indigo → important colonial cash crop.\nIndigo Revolt (1859–60) → peasant resistance in Bengal.\nSynthetic dyes → major challenge to natural indigo.\nColonial plantation system → associated with coercive cultivation practices."},
{"no":34,"text":"Wellesley established Fort William College at Calcutta because:","A":"He was asked by the Board of Directors to do so","B":"He wanted to revive interest in Oriental learning","C":"He wanted to provide William Carey and associates employment","D":"He wanted to train British civilians for administrative purposes","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Fort William College","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Lord Wellesley established Fort William College in 1800.\nIts major purpose was to train young British officials in Indian languages, laws and administrative matters.\nThis was intended to improve their ability to administer British territories in India.\nTherefore, option D is correct.","revisionPoints":"Fort William College → established in 1800.\nLord Wellesley → Governor-General.\nPurpose → training East India Company officials.\nIndian languages and administrative knowledge were important components."},
{"no":35,"text":"“Ulgulan” or the Great Tumult refers to:","A":"Revolt of 1857","B":"Moplah Rebellion of 1921","C":"Indigo Revolt of 1859–60","D":"Birsa Munda's Revolt of 1899–1900","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Birsa Munda","Tribal Revolts","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Ulgulan literally refers to the “Great Tumult.”\nIt is associated with the tribal uprising led by Birsa Munda.\nThe movement occurred in the Chotanagpur region.\nIt challenged colonial authority and exploitative land and social systems.","revisionPoints":"Birsa Munda → major tribal leader.\nUlgulan → 1899–1900.\nRegion → Chotanagpur.\nMajor issues → land alienation, exploitation and colonial interference.\nTribal movements are important in modern Indian history."},
{"no":36,"text":"With reference to scholars/literatures of ancient India:\nPanini is associated with Pushyamitra Shunga.\nAmarasimha is associated with Harshavardhana.\nKalidasa is associated with Chandragupta II.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Ancient Scholars","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Kalidasa is traditionally associated with the Gupta period and especially Chandragupta II.\nThe source does not associate Panini with Pushyamitra Shunga.\nThe source also does not accept the Amarasimha–Harshavardhana association.\nTherefore, only statement 3 is correct according to the source.","revisionPoints":"Panini → Ashtadhyayi.\nKalidasa → major Sanskrit poet and dramatist.\nGupta period → often described as a major period of Sanskrit literary development.\nAmarasimha → traditionally associated with Amarakosha."},
{"no":37,"text":"In the context of recent advances in human reproductive technology, Pronuclear Transfer is used for:","A":"Fertilization of egg in vitro by donor sperm","B":"Genetic modification of sperm-producing cells","C":"Development of stem cells into functional embryos","D":"Prevention of mitochondrial diseases in offspring","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["Pronuclear Transfer","Mitochondrial Disease","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Pronuclear transfer is a reproductive technology associated with mitochondrial replacement.\nIt involves transferring genetic material between eggs/embryos to reduce the transmission of harmful mitochondrial mutations.\nThe objective is to help prevent certain mitochondrial diseases from being passed to offspring.\nHence, option D is correct.","revisionPoints":"Mitochondria → contain their own DNA.\nMitochondrial DNA is generally inherited maternally.\nMitochondrial replacement techniques aim to reduce transmission of mitochondrial disorders.\nPronuclear transfer → reproductive biotechnology."},
{"no":38,"text":"With the present state of development, Artificial Intelligence can effectively do which of the following?\nBring down electricity consumption in industrial units\nCreate meaningful short stories and songs\nDisease diagnosis\nText-to-Speech conversion\nWireless transmission of electrical energy","A":"1, 2, 3 and 5 only","B":"1, 3 and 4 only","C":"2, 4 and 5 only","D":"1, 2, 3, 4 and 5","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Artificial Intelligence","tags":["Artificial Intelligence","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"AI can optimize industrial operations and potentially reduce energy consumption.\nAI-based systems can assist in medical diagnosis.\nText-to-Speech is an established AI-related application.\nWireless transmission of electrical energy is a different technological field and is not itself an AI capability.\nThe source answer is B.","revisionPoints":"AI → systems capable of performing tasks requiring aspects of intelligence.\nApplications → diagnosis, optimization, speech processing.\nAI ≠ every advanced technology.\nDistinguish AI applications from unrelated physical technologies."},
{"no":39,"text":"With reference to Visible Light Communication (VLC), which statements are correct?\nVLC uses electromagnetic spectrum wavelengths 375 to 780 nm.\nVLC is known as long-range optical wireless communication.\nVLC can transmit large amounts of data faster than Bluetooth.\nVLC has no electromagnetic interference.","A":"1, 2 and 3 only","B":"1, 2 and 4 only","C":"1, 3 and 4 only","D":"2, 3 and 4 only","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["Visible Light Communication","LiFi","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"VLC uses the visible-light portion of the electromagnetic spectrum.\nIt can provide high-speed data transmission using modulated light.\nIt is not generally classified as long-range optical wireless communication.\nThe source identifies 1, 3 and 4 as correct.","revisionPoints":"VLC → communication using visible light.\nUses LEDs/light sources for data transmission.\nRequires line-of-sight or suitable optical propagation.\nDifferent from conventional radio-frequency wireless communication.\nPotential applications → indoor communication and smart lighting."},
{"no":40,"text":"With reference to Blockchain Technology, consider the following statements:\nIt is a public ledger that everyone can inspect, but no single user controls.\nIts structure and design mean that all data in it are about cryptocurrency only.\nApplications depending on basic blockchain features can be developed without anybody's permission.","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 only","D":"1 and 3 only","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["Blockchain","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Blockchain can function as a distributed ledger, where records are maintained across multiple participants.\nBlockchain technology is not limited to cryptocurrency; it can support other applications.\nPermissionless blockchain systems can allow developers to build applications without requiring permission from a central authority.\nTherefore, statements 1 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Blockchain → distributed ledger technology.\nData are stored in linked blocks.\nCryptocurrencies are only one application of blockchain.\nBlockchain can support smart contracts and other decentralized applications.\nPermissioned and permissionless blockchains are different models.\nUPSC – Questions 41–60"},
{"no":41,"text":"With reference to carbon nanotubes, consider the following statements:\nThey can be used as carriers of drugs and antigens in the human body.\nThey can be made into artificial blood capillaries for an injured part of the human body.\nThey can be used in biochemical sensors.\nCarbon nanotubes are biodegradable.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2, 3 and 4 only","C":"1, 3 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Materials Science","tags":["Carbon Nanotubes","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Carbon nanotubes have potential biomedical applications, including drug and antigen delivery.\nThey can also be used in biochemical sensing.\nThe source marks statement 4 as correct and therefore selects 1, 3 and 4.\nHence, the answer is C.","revisionPoints":"Carbon nanotubes → cylindrical structures made of carbon.\nImportant properties → high strength and electrical conductivity.\nApplications → sensors, electronics and biomedical technologies.\nNanotechnology has applications across medicine, energy and materials science."},
{"no":42,"text":"Consider the following activities:\nSpraying pesticides on a crop field\nInspecting craters of active volcanoes\nCollecting breath samples from spouting whales for DNA analysis\nAt the present level of technology, which activities can be successfully carried out using drones?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Defence Technology","tags":["Drones","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Drones can be equipped for precision agriculture, including pesticide spraying.\nThey can collect imagery and data from hazardous areas such as active volcanoes.\nSpecialized drones can collect biological samples, including whale breath samples.\nTherefore, all three activities are possible with appropriate drone technology.","revisionPoints":"Drones → unmanned aerial vehicles.\nAgriculture → spraying, monitoring and crop assessment.\nDisaster/volcano studies → remote data collection.\nWildlife research → non-invasive sampling and observation.\nMajor advantage → access to difficult or dangerous locations."},
{"no":43,"text":"“The experiment will employ a trio of spacecraft flying in formation in the shape of an equilateral triangle that has sides one million kilometres long, with lasers shining between the craft.”\nThe experiment refers to:","A":"Voyager-2","B":"New Horizons","C":"LISA Pathfinder","D":"Evolved LISA","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["LISA","Gravitational Waves","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"LISA stands for Laser Interferometer Space Antenna.\nThe proposed system uses spacecraft separated by enormous distances in a triangular formation.\nLasers are used to detect extremely small changes in distance caused by gravitational waves.\nThe source identifies Evolved LISA as the answer.","revisionPoints":"LISA → space-based gravitational-wave observatory concept.\nUses laser interferometry.\nGravitational waves → ripples in spacetime.\nSpace-based detectors complement ground-based observatories.\nDo not confuse LISA with planetary exploration missions such as Voyager and New Horizons."},
{"no":44,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nGenetic changes can be introduced in cells that produce eggs or sperms of a prospective parent.\nA person's genome can be edited before birth at the early embryonic stage.\nHuman induced pluripotent stem cells can be injected into the embryo of a pig.\nWhich statements are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["Gene Editing","Stem Cells","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Genetic engineering techniques can target germline cells.\nGenome editing can potentially be performed at an early embryonic stage.\nResearch has also investigated the use of human induced pluripotent stem cells in animal embryos.\nThe source identifies all three statements as correct.","revisionPoints":"Germline editing → genetic changes potentially passed to future generations.\nSomatic editing → changes restricted to treated individual cells/tissues.\niPSCs → adult cells reprogrammed to a pluripotent state.\nEmbryonic gene editing raises significant ethical and regulatory issues."},
{"no":45,"text":"What is the importance of using Pneumococcal Conjugate Vaccines (PCV) in India?\nThese vaccines are effective against pneumonia as well as meningitis and sepsis.\nDependence on antibiotics that are not effective against drug-resistant bacteria can be reduced.\nThese vaccines have no side effects and cause no allergic reactions.","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["Vaccines","Pneumonia","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Pneumococcal disease can cause pneumonia, meningitis and bloodstream infections.\nVaccination can reduce disease burden and consequently reduce the need for antibiotics.\nStatement 3 is incorrect because no vaccine can generally be described as having absolutely no side effects or allergic reactions.\nHence, 1 and 2 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Pneumococcus → Streptococcus pneumoniae.\nDiseases → pneumonia, meningitis, sepsis and other infections.\nPCV → conjugate vaccine.\nVaccination can contribute to reducing antimicrobial resistance pressure."},
{"no":46,"text":"In India, the term Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) is used in the context of:","A":"Digital security infrastructure","B":"Food security infrastructure","C":"Healthcare and education infrastructure","D":"Telecommunication and transportation infrastructure","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["Public Key Infrastructure","Digital Security","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"PKI is a framework used to establish digital identity and secure electronic communication.\nIt uses public-key cryptography and digital certificates.\nIt supports authentication, encryption and digital signatures.\nTherefore, option A is correct.","revisionPoints":"PKI → Public Key Infrastructure.\nDigital certificate → links identity with a public key.\nDigital signatures → authentication and integrity.\nPublic-key cryptography → uses public and private keys."},
{"no":47,"text":"Which statements correctly describe the general difference between plant and animal cells?\nPlant cells have cellulose cell walls whereas animal cells do not.\nPlant cells do not have a plasma membrane unlike animal cells.\nMature plant cells have one large vacuole whereas animal cells have many small vacuoles.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Cell Biology","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Plant cells generally possess a cellulose cell wall; animal cells do not.\nPlant cells do have a plasma membrane, making statement 2 incorrect.\nMature plant cells commonly have a large central vacuole.\nTherefore, statements 1 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Plant cell → cell wall + plasma membrane.\nCell wall → mainly cellulose.\nLarge central vacuole → characteristic of mature plant cells.\nAnimal cells → lack cell wall."},
{"no":48,"text":"Which of the following are reasons/factors for exposure to benzene pollution?\nAutomobile exhaust\nTobacco smoke\nWood burning\nUsing varnished wooden furniture\nUsing products made of polyurethane","A":"1, 2 and 3 only","B":"2 and 4 only","C":"1, 3 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3, 4 and 5","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["Benzene","Air Pollution","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Benzene is a volatile organic compound.\nImportant sources include vehicle exhaust, tobacco smoke and combustion of wood.\nThe source identifies these three as the relevant exposure factors.\nHence, option A is correct.","revisionPoints":"Benzene → volatile organic compound.\nMajor exposure → tobacco smoke and combustion-related emissions.\nLong-term exposure → serious health concerns.\nAir pollution questions often require distinguishing primary pollutants from secondary pollutants."},
{"no":49,"text":"If another global financial crisis happens in the near future, which action/policy is most likely to give some immunity to India?\nNot depending on short-term foreign borrowings\nOpening up to more foreign banks\nMaintaining full capital account convertibility","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Markets","tags":["Global Financial Crisis","Capital Account Convertibility","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Dependence on short-term foreign borrowing can increase vulnerability to sudden capital outflows.\nReducing such dependence can provide greater resilience during an external financial shock.\nGreater openness to foreign banks or full capital-account convertibility would not necessarily provide immunity from global financial shocks.\nHence, the source answer is 1 only.","revisionPoints":"Short-term external debt → vulnerability to sudden capital outflows.\nCapital account → cross-border financial transactions.\nCapital account convertibility → greater freedom for capital movements.\nExternal financial shocks can affect exchange rates, capital flows and financial stability."},
{"no":50,"text":"If you withdraw ₹1,00,000 in cash from your Demand Deposit Account at your bank, the immediate effect on aggregate money supply will be:","A":"Reduce it by ₹1,00,000","B":"Increase it by ₹1,00,000","C":"Increase it by more than ₹1,00,000","D":"Leave it unchanged","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking","tags":["Money Supply","Banking","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"A demand deposit is part of the money supply.\nWhen you withdraw cash, the composition of money changes from bank deposit to currency held by the public.\nThe amount of money itself does not immediately change merely because its form changes.\nTherefore, aggregate money supply remains unchanged.","revisionPoints":"Money supply includes currency with public and certain bank deposits.\nCash withdrawal → conversion of deposit money into currency.\nMere conversion between components does not necessarily change total money supply.\nCredit creation by banks is a separate mechanism affecting money supply."},
{"no":51,"text":"With reference to Foreign Direct Investment (FDI) in India, which is its major characteristic?","A":"Investment through capital instruments essentially in a listed company","B":"Largely non-debt creating capital flow","C":"Investment involving debt-servicing","D":"Investment made by foreign institutional investors in government securities","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Trade","tags":["FDI","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"FDI represents investment by a foreign investor in an enterprise with a lasting interest/control-related element.\nUnlike borrowing, FDI generally does not create a conventional debt obligation requiring repayment of principal and interest.\nTherefore, it is considered a non-debt creating capital flow.","revisionPoints":"FDI → foreign investment with lasting interest.\nFDI is different from foreign portfolio investment.\nFDI → generally non-debt creating.\nExternal commercial borrowing → debt-creating flow."},
{"no":52,"text":"With reference to India's international trade, consider the following statements:\nIndia's merchandise exports are less than its merchandise imports.\nIndia's imports of iron and steel, chemicals, fertilisers and machinery have decreased in recent years.\nIndia's exports of services are more than its imports of services.\nIndia suffers from an overall trade/current account deficit.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 4 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 3 and 4 only","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Trade","tags":["Trade Deficit","Exports","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"The source identifies statements 1 and 2 as correct.\nIndia has historically had a merchandise trade deficit, with merchandise imports exceeding merchandise exports.\nThe question's source also treats the specified categories of imports as having decreased.\nStatements 3 and 4 are therefore not accepted in the source answer.","revisionPoints":"Merchandise trade → trade in physical goods.\nServices trade → trade in services.\nTrade balance → exports minus imports of goods.\nCurrent account includes goods, services, income and transfers."},
{"no":53,"text":"The term West Texas Intermediate (WTI) refers to a grade of:","A":"Crude oil","B":"Bullion","C":"Rare earth elements","D":"Uranium","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Energy Sector Regulation","tags":["Crude Oil","WTI","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"West Texas Intermediate is a benchmark grade of crude oil.\nIt is widely used as a reference price in global oil markets.\nWTI is known for its relatively light and sweet characteristics.\nHence, option A is correct.","revisionPoints":"WTI → crude oil benchmark.\nBrent → another major crude oil benchmark.\n“Light” → relatively lower density.\n“Sweet” → relatively low sulphur content."},
{"no":54,"text":"In the context of the Indian economy, non-financial debt includes:\nHousing loans owed by households\nAmounts outstanding on credit cards\nTreasury bills","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking","tags":["Household Debt","Treasury Bills","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"The source classifies all three as components of non-financial debt.\nHousehold borrowing such as housing loans represents liabilities of households.\nCredit-card outstanding amounts also represent household debt.\nGovernment securities such as Treasury bills represent government borrowing.\nHence, all three are included according to the source.","revisionPoints":"Non-financial sector → households, non-financial corporations and government.\nFinancial-sector liabilities are treated separately in debt classifications.\nTreasury bills → short-term government borrowing instruments.\nHousehold debt includes housing and consumer borrowing."},
{"no":55,"text":"In India, why are some nuclear reactors kept under IAEA Safeguards while others are not?","A":"Some use uranium and others use thorium.","B":"Some use imported uranium and others use domestic supplies.","C":"Some are operated by foreign enterprises and others by domestic enterprises.","D":"Some are State-owned and others privately owned.","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Nuclear Technology","tags":["IAEA Safeguards","Nuclear Reactors","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"India's civilian nuclear facilities are divided into safeguarded and unsafeguarded categories.\nInternational safeguards are associated with civilian facilities using internationally supplied nuclear material under applicable agreements.\nIndigenous strategic facilities can remain outside those safeguards.\nThe source identifies option B.","revisionPoints":"IAEA → International Atomic Energy Agency.\nSafeguards → verification that nuclear material is not diverted from peaceful uses.\nIndia → has both safeguarded and unsafeguarded nuclear facilities.\nInternational agreements determine the scope of safeguards."},
{"no":56,"text":"With reference to Trade-Related Investment Measures (TRIMs), which statements are correct?\nQuantitative restrictions on imports by foreign investors are prohibited.\nThey apply to investment measures related to trade in both goods and services.\nThey are not concerned with regulation of foreign investment.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Trade","tags":["TRIMS","WTO","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"TRIMs are WTO rules dealing with investment measures that affect trade in goods.\nCertain trade-distorting investment requirements are prohibited.\nTRIMs are not a general code regulating foreign investment itself.\nStatement 2 is incorrect because the agreement concerns goods, not services.","revisionPoints":"TRIMs → Trade-Related Investment Measures.\nWTO agreement.\nFocus → investment measures affecting trade in goods.\nLocal-content requirements can fall within prohibited measures.\nTRIMs ≠ comprehensive foreign investment regulation."},
{"no":57,"text":"If RBI decides to adopt an expansionist monetary policy, which would it not do?\nCut and optimize the Statutory Liquidity Ratio (SLR)\nIncrease the Marginal Standing Facility (MSF) Rate\nCut the Bank Rate and Repo Rate","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["Monetary Policy","RBI","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Expansionary monetary policy aims to increase liquidity and encourage borrowing.\nReducing policy rates such as the Repo Rate generally supports easier credit conditions.\nIncreasing the MSF rate would make emergency borrowing from RBI more expensive and is therefore inconsistent with an expansionary stance.\nHence, statement 2 is the answer.","revisionPoints":"Expansionary monetary policy → increase liquidity/credit.\nRepo Rate ↓ → generally encourages borrowing.\nMSF Rate ↑ → tighter borrowing conditions.\nSLR → portion of bank liabilities maintained in specified liquid assets.\nRBI uses monetary tools to influence liquidity and credit conditions."},
{"no":58,"text":"With reference to the Indian economy after the 1991 economic liberalization, consider:\nWorker productivity increased in urban areas while decreasing in rural areas.\nPercentage share of rural areas in workforce steadily increased.\nIn rural areas, growth in the non-farm economy increased.\nGrowth rate in rural employment decreased.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 and 4 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 4 only","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Economic Liberalization","Employment","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"The source identifies statements 3 and 4 as correct.\nRural India experienced growth in non-farm economic activities.\nAt the same time, the growth rate of rural employment declined.\nStatements 1 and 2 are not accepted by the source.","revisionPoints":"1991 reforms → liberalization, privatization and globalization.\nRural economy → includes both farm and non-farm sectors.\nRural non-farm sector → manufacturing, construction and services.\nEmployment growth and productivity can move differently across sectors."},
{"no":59,"text":"Consider the following statements about District Central Cooperative Banks (DCCBs):\nIn short-term credit delivery to agriculture, DCCBs deliver more credit than Scheduled Commercial Banks and Regional Rural Banks.\nOne important function of DCCBs is to provide funds to Primary Agricultural Credit Societies (PACS).","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking","tags":["Cooperative Banks","Agricultural Credit","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"DCCBs form an important part of the short-term cooperative credit structure.\nThey provide financial support to Primary Agricultural Credit Societies (PACS).\nThe source does not accept the claim that DCCBs deliver more short-term agricultural credit than both Scheduled Commercial Banks and RRBs.\nTherefore, only statement 2 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Cooperative credit structure → three-tier structure in many states.\nPACS → grassroots-level cooperative credit institutions.\nDCCB → district-level cooperative bank.\nState Cooperative Bank → apex level.\nRRBs and commercial banks are separate institutional channels."},
{"no":60,"text":"In India, under cyber insurance for individuals, which benefits are generally covered in addition to payment for loss of funds?\nCost of restoration of computer system after malware disruption\nCost of a new computer if deliberately damaged by a miscreant\nCost of hiring a specialized consultant to minimize loss in cyber extortion\nCost of defence in court if a third party files a suit","A":"1, 2 and 4 only","B":"1, 3 and 4 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Insurance","tags":["Cyber Insurance","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Cyber insurance can cover costs related to malware/system restoration.\nIt may cover professional assistance associated with cyber extortion.\nCertain policies can also cover legal defence costs arising from specified cyber-related claims.\nThe source does not include the cost of replacing a computer deliberately damaged by a miscreant.\nHence, 1, 3 and 4 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Cyber insurance → protection against specified cyber-related financial risks.\nPossible coverage → data/system restoration.\nCyber extortion → specialized response costs may be covered.\nLegal defence → may be included depending on policy terms.\nCoverage differs between insurance products; policy wording matters."},
{"no":61,"text":"In India, which of the following can be considered as public investment in agriculture?\nFixing Minimum Support Price (MSP) for agricultural produce of all crops\nComputerization of Primary Agricultural Credit Societies\nSocial Capital development\nFree electricity supply to farmers\nWaiver of agricultural loans by the banking system\nSetting up of cold storage facilities by governments","A":"1, 2 and 5 only","B":"1, 3, 4 and 5 only","C":"2, 3 and 6 only","D":"1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Public Investment","Agriculture","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Public investment refers to government spending that creates or improves productive/public infrastructure or capabilities.\nComputerization of PACS can strengthen agricultural credit infrastructure.\nSocial capital development can support rural economic development.\nGovernment cold storage facilities are physical infrastructure supporting agriculture.\nMSP, free electricity and loan waivers are generally policy/support measures rather than public investment in this context.","revisionPoints":"Public investment → creation of productive/public assets and capabilities.\nAgricultural infrastructure → storage, irrigation, roads, markets and technology.\nMSP → price-support mechanism.\nLoan waiver → relief measure, not capital investment.\nPACS → grassroots cooperative credit institutions."},
{"no":62,"text":"What is the importance of the Interest Coverage Ratio of a firm in India?\nIt helps in understanding the present risk of a firm that a bank is going to give a loan to.\nIt helps in evaluating the emerging risk of a firm that a bank is going to give a loan to.\nThe higher the borrowing firm's Interest Coverage Ratio, the worse its ability to service debt.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Markets","tags":["Interest Coverage Ratio","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Interest Coverage Ratio indicates how comfortably a firm can meet its interest obligations from its operating earnings.\nIt is useful for assessing the credit risk associated with lending to a firm.\nA higher ratio generally indicates a stronger ability to service interest obligations.\nTherefore, statement 3 is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Interest Coverage Ratio → measure of debt-servicing capacity.\nHigher ratio → generally greater ability to pay interest.\nLower ratio → greater financial risk.\nImportant for banks and lenders while assessing borrowers."},
{"no":63,"text":"Which factors/policies were affecting the price of rice in India in the recent past?\nMinimum Support Price\nGovernment's trading\nGovernment's stockpiling\nConsumer subsidies","A":"1, 2 and 4 only","B":"1, 3 and 4 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["MSP","Rice","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"MSP can influence procurement prices and market expectations.\nGovernment procurement and trading affect the availability of rice in markets.\nStockpiling changes the amount of grain available for market release.\nConsumer subsidies can influence effective prices faced by consumers.\nTherefore, all four factors are relevant.","revisionPoints":"MSP → minimum procurement price framework.\nGovernment procurement → affects foodgrain stocks.\nBuffer stocks → help manage food security and price stability.\nConsumer subsidies → reduce effective cost for eligible consumers."},
{"no":64,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe value of Indo-Sri Lanka trade has consistently increased in the last decade.\n“Textile and textile articles” constitute an important item of trade between India and Bangladesh.\nIn the last five years, Nepal has been the largest trading partner of India in South Asia.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Trade","tags":["South Asia Trade","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"The source identifies only statement 2 as correct.\nTextile and textile articles are an important component of India-Bangladesh trade.\nThe word “consistently” makes statement 1 incorrect in the source's framing.\nNepal is not identified as India's largest South Asian trading partner in the source.","revisionPoints":"India-Bangladesh → important bilateral trade relationship.\nTextiles → major component of regional trade.\nIndia has significant economic relations with several South Asian neighbours.\nBe careful with absolute terms such as “consistently” and “largest.”"},
{"no":65,"text":"In which group are all four countries members of G20?","A":"Argentina, Mexico, South Africa and Turkey","B":"Australia, Canada, Malaysia and New Zealand","C":"Brazil, Iran, Saudi Arabia and Vietnam","D":"Indonesia, Japan, Singapore and South Korea","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Organizations","tags":["G20","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Argentina, Mexico, South Africa and Türkiye are all members of the G20.\nThe other options contain countries that are not G20 members.\nTherefore, option A is correct.","revisionPoints":"G20 → major forum for international economic cooperation.\nIncludes major advanced and emerging economies.\nSouth Africa → African G20 member.\nArgentina, Mexico and Türkiye → G20 members.\nMembership is distinct from G7 membership."},
{"no":66,"text":"Under the Kisan Credit Card (KCC) scheme, short-term credit support is given for which purposes?\nWorking capital for maintenance of farm assets\nPurchase of combine harvesters, tractors and mini trucks\nConsumption requirements of farm households\nPost-harvest expenses\nConstruction of family house and setting up of village cold storage facility","A":"1, 2 and 5 only","B":"1, 3 and 4 only","C":"2, 3, 4 and 5 only","D":"1, 2, 3, 4 and 5","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Kisan Credit Card","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"KCC provides short-term credit for working capital related to agriculture.\nIt can cover maintenance of farm assets.\nIt can meet specified household consumption requirements.\nIt also covers post-harvest expenses.\nConstruction of a family house and village cold-storage facilities are not included in this short-term credit category.","revisionPoints":"KCC → agricultural credit facility.\nSupports crop production and related short-term needs.\nPost-harvest expenses → eligible agricultural credit requirement.\nLong-term capital expenditure → generally distinguished from short-term KCC purposes."},
{"no":67,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe weightage of food in CPI is higher than that in WPI.\nWPI does not capture changes in prices of services, whereas CPI does.\nRBI has adopted WPI as its key measure of inflation for deciding key policy rates.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["CPI","WPI","Inflation","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Food has a significant weight in CPI, particularly because CPI reflects consumer expenditure patterns.\nWPI primarily tracks prices of goods and does not cover services in the same manner as CPI.\nRBI uses CPI inflation as its headline inflation target framework, not WPI.\nTherefore, statements 1 and 2 are correct.","revisionPoints":"CPI → consumer prices.\nWPI → wholesale prices of goods.\nCPI includes services; WPI does not comprehensively cover services.\nRBI's inflation-targeting framework uses CPI.\nInflation indices measure different stages of the economy."},
{"no":68,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nRiver\nFlows into\n1. Mekong\nAndaman Sea\n2. Thames\nIrish Sea\n3. Volga\nCaspian Sea\n4. Zambezi\nIndian Ocean","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"3 and 4 only","D":"1, 2 and 4 only","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Rivers","World Geography","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"The Volga flows into the Caspian Sea.\nThe Zambezi flows into the Indian Ocean.\nThe Mekong reaches the South China Sea, not the Andaman Sea.\nThe Thames flows into the North Sea, not the Irish Sea.","revisionPoints":"Mekong → South China Sea.\nThames → North Sea.\nVolga → Caspian Sea.\nZambezi → Indian Ocean.\nRiver-mouth questions require careful knowledge of seas and drainage basins."},
{"no":69,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nIn all cereals, pulses and oilseeds, procurement at MSP is unlimited in any State/UT.\nFor cereals and pulses, MSP is fixed at a level to which market price will never rise.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["MSP","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"MSP does not mean unlimited procurement of all crops in every State/UT.\nMarket prices can rise above MSP depending on demand and supply.\nTherefore, neither statement is correct.\nThe source answer is D.","revisionPoints":"MSP → announced minimum support price for specified crops.\nMSP ≠ guaranteed unlimited procurement everywhere.\nMarket price can be above or below MSP.\nProcurement depends on crop, agency, region and government policy."},
{"no":70,"text":"With reference to the Indian economy:\nCommercial Paper is a short-term unsecured promissory note.\nCertificate of Deposit is a long-term instrument issued by RBI to a corporation.\nCall Money is short-term finance used for interbank transactions.\nZero-Coupon Bonds are interest-bearing short-term bonds issued by Scheduled Commercial Banks to corporations.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"4 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"2, 3 and 4 only","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Markets","tags":["Money Market Instruments","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Commercial Paper (CP) → short-term unsecured money-market instrument.\nCall Money → very short-term borrowing/lending, particularly in the interbank market.\nCertificate of Deposit is a short-term money-market instrument, not a long-term RBI instrument issued to corporations.\nZero-coupon bonds do not pay periodic interest in the conventional sense.","revisionPoints":"CP → short-term unsecured promissory note.\nCD → negotiable money-market instrument.\nCall Money → very short-term funds.\nZero-coupon bond → issued at discount and redeemed at face value.\nMoney market → short-term financial instruments."},
{"no":71,"text":"With reference to the cultural history of India, consider the pairs:\nTerm\nDescription\n1. Parivrajaka\nRenunciant and wanderer\n2. Shramana\nPriest with a high status\n3. Upasaka\nLay follower of Buddhism","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"1 and 3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Buddhism","Ancient Terminology","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Parivrajaka refers to a renunciant or wandering ascetic.\nUpasaka refers to a lay follower of Buddhism.\nShramana refers broadly to an ascetic/renunciant tradition, not a high-status priest.\nHence, pairs 1 and 3 are correctly matched.","revisionPoints":"Parivrajaka → wandering renunciant.\nShramana → non-Vedic ascetic traditions.\nUpasaka → male lay Buddhist follower.\nUpasika → female lay Buddhist follower.\nShramana traditions include Buddhism and Jainism."},
{"no":72,"text":"With reference to Indian elephants, consider:\nThe leader of an elephant group is a female.\nMaximum gestation period can be 22 months.\nAn elephant can normally go on calving till age 40 years only.\nAmong Indian States, highest elephant population is in Kerala.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 4 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 3 and 4 only","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Elephants","Wildlife","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Elephant herds are generally matriarchal, with an experienced female leading the group.\nElephant gestation can last around 22 months.\nThe statement limiting calving strictly to age 40 is incorrect.\nThe source does not identify Kerala as having the highest elephant population.","revisionPoints":"Elephant herd → generally led by a female matriarch.\nGestation → about 22 months.\nAsian elephant → Elephas maximus.\nElephant populations occur across several Indian States.\nHabitat loss and fragmentation are major conservation concerns."},
{"no":73,"text":"Which Protected Areas are located in the Cauvery basin?\nNagarhole National Park\nPapikonda National Park\nSathyamangalam Tiger Reserve\nWayanad Wildlife Sanctuary","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 and 4 only","C":"1, 3 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Protected Areas","Cauvery Basin","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Nagarhole National Park is associated with the Cauvery river system.\nSathyamangalam Tiger Reserve lies within the broader Cauvery landscape.\nWayanad Wildlife Sanctuary is also connected to the Cauvery basin through its drainage system.\nPapikonda is associated with the Godavari basin, not Cauvery.","revisionPoints":"Cauvery → major peninsular river.\nImportant protected areas → Nagarhole, Sathyamangalam and Wayanad landscapes.\nPapikonda → Godavari basin.\nBasin-based mapping is important for UPSC geography/environment questions."},
{"no":74,"text":"With reference to India's biodiversity, Ceylon frogmouth, Coppersmith barbet, Gray-chinned minivet and White-throated redstart are:","A":"Birds","B":"Primates","C":"Reptiles","D":"Amphibians","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Birds","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"All four names refer to bird species.\nThe question tests identification of species based on common names.\nTherefore, option A is correct.","revisionPoints":"Ceylon frogmouth → bird.\nCoppersmith barbet → bird.\nGray-chinned minivet → bird.\nWhite-throated redstart → bird.\nSpecies identification can be linked with habitat and geographic distribution."},
{"no":75,"text":"Which protected area is well-known for conservation of a subspecies of the Indian swamp deer (Barasingha) that thrives on hard ground and is exclusively graminivorous?","A":"Kanha National Park","B":"Manas National Park","C":"Mudumalai Wildlife Sanctuary","D":"Tal Chhapar Wildlife Sanctuary","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Barasingha","Kanha National Park","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Kanha National Park is well known for conservation of the hard-ground swamp deer or Barasingha.\nThe hard-ground Barasingha is an important conservation species of the Kanha landscape.\nTherefore, option A is correct.","revisionPoints":"Barasingha → swamp deer.\nKanha → famous for hard-ground Barasingha conservation.\nGraminivorous → primarily grass-eating.\nProtected areas often have flagship species associated with them."},
{"no":76,"text":"Steel slag can be used for:\nConstruction of base road\nImprovement of agricultural soil\nProduction of cement","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Sustainable Development","tags":["Steel Slag","Waste Utilization","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Steel slag is an industrial by-product with potential applications in road construction.\nIts mineral composition can allow certain uses in soil improvement.\nIt can also be used as a raw material/component in cement production.\nHence, all three applications are identified by the source.","revisionPoints":"Steel slag → by-product of steel manufacturing.\nReuse → reduces industrial waste.\nApplications → roads, construction materials and cement.\nIndustrial waste utilization supports circular-economy principles."},
{"no":77,"text":"Which are the most likely places to find musk deer in their natural habitat?\nAskot Wildlife Sanctuary\nGangotri National Park\nKishanpur Wildlife Sanctuary\nManas National Park","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"3 and 4 only","D":"1 and 4 only","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Musk Deer","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Musk deer are associated with high-altitude Himalayan habitats.\nAskot Wildlife Sanctuary and Gangotri National Park provide suitable Himalayan habitat.\nKishanpur and Manas are not the typical high-altitude habitats associated with musk deer.\nTherefore, 1 and 2 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Musk deer → Himalayan/high-altitude species.\nHabitat → alpine and sub-alpine environments.\nAskot → Uttarakhand.\nGangotri → Uttarakhand Himalayas.\nHabitat-based species identification is important for mapping questions."},
{"no":78,"text":"In rural road construction, which are preferred for environmental sustainability or reducing carbon footprint?\nCopper slag\nCold mix asphalt technology\nGeotextiles\nHot mix asphalt technology\nPortland cement","A":"1, 2 and 3 only","B":"2, 3 and 4 only","C":"4 and 5 only","D":"1 and 5 only","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Sustainable Development","tags":["Rural Roads","Sustainable Materials","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Copper slag can substitute certain conventional construction materials.\nCold mix asphalt can reduce energy requirements compared with conventional hot mixing.\nGeotextiles can improve road performance and reduce material requirements in suitable applications.\nHot mix asphalt and Portland cement are more energy-intensive options in this context.","revisionPoints":"Cold mix technology → lower-temperature road construction.\nGeotextiles → improve soil/road structural performance.\nIndustrial by-products → potential construction materials.\nSustainable infrastructure → resource efficiency + lower emissions."},
{"no":79,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nCoal ash contains arsenic, lead and mercury.\nCoal-fired power plants release sulphur dioxide and oxides of nitrogen.\nHigh ash content is observed in Indian coal.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["Coal Ash","Air Pollution","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Coal ash can contain trace toxic elements such as arsenic, lead and mercury.\nCoal combustion can release SO₂ and nitrogen oxides, depending on fuel and emission controls.\nIndian coal is known for relatively high ash content.\nTherefore, all three statements are correct.","revisionPoints":"Coal ash → solid residue from coal combustion.\nHeavy/toxic elements → environmental and health concerns.\nSO₂ → contributes to acid deposition and air pollution.\nNOx → contributes to ozone formation and other air-quality problems.\nIndian coal → relatively high ash content."},
{"no":80,"text":"What is the use of biochar in farming?\nBiochar can be used as part of the growing medium in vertical farming.\nBiochar in the growing medium promotes growth of nitrogen-fixing microorganisms.\nBiochar in the growing medium enables it to retain water for longer.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Sustainable Development","tags":["Biochar","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Biochar is a carbon-rich material produced through the thermal decomposition of biomass.\nIt can be incorporated into growing media, including certain vertical farming systems.\nIts porous structure can improve water retention.\nIt can also provide a suitable environment for beneficial microorganisms.\nThe source identifies all three statements as correct.","revisionPoints":"Biochar → stable carbon-rich material.\nProduced from biomass under limited-oxygen conditions.\nCan improve soil/growing-medium properties.\nPotential benefits → water retention, nutrient management and carbon storage.\nBiochar is associated with carbon sequestration and circular use of biomass."},
{"no":81,"text":"If a particular plant species is placed under Schedule VI of the Wildlife (Protection) Act, 1972, what is the implication?","A":"A licence is required to cultivate that plant.","B":"Such a plant cannot be cultivated under any circumstances.","C":"It is a Genetically Modified crop plant.","D":"Such a plant is invasive and harmful to the ecosystem.","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Wildlife Protection Act","Plant Species","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Schedule VI deals with specified plants requiring protection under the Wildlife (Protection) Act.\nCultivation of such protected plants is regulated.\nA person requires the prescribed licence/permission for cultivation.\nTherefore, option A is correct.","revisionPoints":"Wildlife Protection Act → protection of wildlife, plants and habitats.\nSchedule VI → specified protected plant species.\nProtected plants → cultivation and trade are regulated.\nDo not confuse protected plants with genetically modified crops."},
{"no":82,"text":"With reference to the Gupta period, the towns Ghantasala, Kadura and Chaul were well known as:","A":"Ports handling foreign trade","B":"Capitals of powerful kingdoms","C":"Places of exquisite stone art and architecture","D":"Important Buddhist pilgrimage centres","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Gupta Empire","Ports","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"These towns were important commercial centres connected with maritime trade.\nTheir geographical position supported overseas trade.\nThey are therefore associated with ports and foreign commerce rather than being primarily capitals or pilgrimage centres.\nHence, option A is correct.","revisionPoints":"Ancient India had extensive maritime trade.\nPorts connected inland production centres with overseas markets.\nIndian Ocean trade linked India with Southeast Asia, West Asia and beyond.\nArchaeological and literary sources help reconstruct ancient trade networks."},
{"no":83,"text":"What are the advantages of zero tillage in agriculture?\nSowing of wheat is possible without burning residue of the previous crop.\nWithout a nursery of rice saplings, direct planting of paddy seeds in wet soil is possible.\nCarbon sequestration in soil is possible.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Zero Tillage","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Zero tillage reduces the need for conventional soil preparation.\nIt allows timely sowing after the previous crop without necessarily burning crop residues.\nDirect-seeded rice can be associated with reduced tillage practices.\nReduced soil disturbance can also contribute to soil carbon sequestration.","revisionPoints":"Zero tillage → minimum disturbance of soil.\nCan reduce fuel and labour requirements.\nHelps reduce crop-residue burning in suitable systems.\nCan improve soil carbon retention.\nPart of conservation agriculture."},
{"no":84,"text":"According to India's National Policy on Biofuels, which can be used as raw materials for biofuel production?\nCassava\nDamaged wheat grains\nGroundnut seeds\nHorse gram\nRotten potatoes\nSugar beet","A":"1, 2, 5 and 6 only","B":"1, 3, 4 and 6 only","C":"2, 3, 4 and 5 only","D":"1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Biofuels","National Policy on Biofuels","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"The source identifies cassava, damaged wheat grains, rotten potatoes and sugar beet as eligible feedstock in the context of the question.\nThese materials can provide starch or sugars suitable for conversion into biofuels.\nThe source does not include groundnut seeds and horse gram in the selected answer.\nTherefore, option A is correct.","revisionPoints":"Biofuel → fuel derived from biological material.\nFeedstock can include agricultural residues and non-food/unused materials.\nEthanol → commonly produced from sugar/starch-rich feedstock.\nBiofuel policy aims at energy diversification and reducing dependence on fossil fuels."},
{"no":85,"text":"Which statement best describes the Social Cost of Carbon?","A":"Long-term damage done by a tonne of CO₂ emissions in a given year.","B":"Requirement of fossil fuels for a country to provide goods and services.","C":"Efforts put in by a climate refugee to adapt to a new place.","D":"Contribution of an individual person to the carbon footprint.","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["Social Cost of Carbon","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Social Cost of Carbon estimates the economic damage associated with an additional unit of CO₂ emissions.\nIt can include impacts on health, agriculture, ecosystems and other climate-related damages.\nIt is generally expressed in monetary terms.\nTherefore, option A is correct.","revisionPoints":"SCC → monetary estimate of climate-related damages.\nUsually expressed per tonne of CO₂.\nUsed in climate policy and cost-benefit analysis.\nDifferent models can produce different estimates."},
{"no":86,"text":"With reference to pulse production in India, consider:\nBlack gram can be cultivated as both kharif and rabi crop.\nGreen gram alone accounts for nearly half of pulse production.\nIn the last three decades, kharif pulse production increased while rabi pulse production decreased.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Pulses","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Black gram (urad) can be cultivated during both kharif and rabi seasons in different regions.\nGreen gram does not account for nearly half of India's total pulse production.\nThe source does not accept the claimed trend for kharif and rabi pulses.\nTherefore, only statement 1 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Pulses → important source of plant protein.\nBlack gram → urad.\nGreen gram → moong.\nPulse cultivation occurs in both kharif and rabi seasons.\nPulses also contribute nitrogen to soil through biological nitrogen fixation."},
{"no":87,"text":"“The crop is subtropical in nature. A hard frost is injurious to it. It requires at least 210 frost-free days and 50–100 cm of rainfall. A light well-drained soil capable of retaining moisture is ideally suited.”\nWhich crop is being described?","A":"Cotton","B":"Jute","C":"Sugarcane","D":"Tea","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Economic Geography","tags":["Cotton","Agro-climatic Conditions","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Cotton is a subtropical crop requiring a long frost-free growing period.\nIt requires moderate rainfall and suitable well-drained soils.\nFrost can severely damage the crop.\nTherefore, the description corresponds to cotton.","revisionPoints":"Cotton → subtropical crop.\nRequires long frost-free period.\nBlack soil is particularly associated with cotton cultivation in India.\nMajor cotton-growing regions include parts of western and central India."},
{"no":88,"text":"With reference to solar water pumps, consider:\nSolar power can be used for running surface pumps and not submersible pumps.\nSolar power can be used for centrifugal pumps and not piston pumps.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Renewable Energy","tags":["Solar Water Pumps","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Solar energy can be used to operate both surface and submersible pumps.\nSolar-powered systems can also operate different pump technologies, including centrifugal and piston-type pumps, depending on the system design.\nTherefore, both statements are incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Solar pumps → use photovoltaic energy to operate water pumps.\nCan be used for irrigation and drinking-water supply.\nSurface pumps → draw water from accessible sources.\nSubmersible pumps → operate below the water surface.\nPump type depends on water source and required head/discharge."},
{"no":89,"text":"With reference to current trends in sugarcane cultivation:\nSubstantial saving in seed material is made when bud-chip settlings are raised in a nursery and transplanted.\nDirect planting of setts gives better germination with single-budded setts than setts with many buds.\nUnder bad weather conditions, single-budded setts have better survival than large setts.\nSugarcane can be cultivated using settlings prepared from tissue culture.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"1 and 4 only","D":"2, 3 and 4 only","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Sugarcane Cultivation","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Bud-chip technology can substantially reduce the amount of planting material required.\nTissue-culture techniques can produce planting material for sugarcane cultivation.\nThe source does not accept statements 2 and 3.\nHence, statements 1 and 4 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Sugarcane → commonly propagated vegetatively through setts.\nBud-chip technology → reduces planting material requirement.\nTissue culture → enables rapid multiplication of disease-free planting material.\nNursery-raised seedlings → can improve planting-material efficiency."},
{"no":90,"text":"In the context of India, which are considered practices of eco-friendly agriculture?\nCrop diversification\nLegume intensification\nTensiometer use\nVertical farming","A":"1, 2 and 3 only","B":"3 only","C":"4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Eco-friendly Agriculture","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Crop diversification can reduce dependence on a single crop and improve resource management.\nLegumes can improve soil fertility through biological nitrogen fixation.\nA tensiometer helps monitor soil moisture and supports efficient irrigation.\nThe source does not include vertical farming in its selected answer.","revisionPoints":"Crop diversification → reduces monoculture risks.\nLegumes → contribute to soil nitrogen through symbiotic fixation.\nTensiometer → measures soil-water tension.\nEfficient irrigation → reduces water wastage.\nSustainable agriculture → resource efficiency + soil health + ecological balance."},
{"no":91,"text":"What are the advantages of fertigation in agriculture?\nControlling the alkalinity of irrigation water is possible.\nEfficient application of rock phosphate and all other phosphatic fertilizers is possible.\nIncreased availability of nutrients to plants is possible.\nReduction in leaching of chemical nutrients is possible.","A":"1, 2 and 3 only","B":"1, 2 and 4 only","C":"1, 3 and 4 only","D":"2, 3 and 4 only","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Fertigation","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Fertigation means applying fertilizers through irrigation water.\nIt allows more precise delivery of nutrients to the root zone.\nIt can increase nutrient availability and improve fertilizer-use efficiency.\nIt can reduce nutrient losses through leaching when properly managed.\nThe source does not include statement 2.","revisionPoints":"Fertigation = fertilizer + irrigation.\nNutrients delivered directly with irrigation water.\nImproves nutrient-use efficiency.\nCan reduce wastage and nutrient losses.\nCommonly associated with drip irrigation."},
{"no":92,"text":"Consider the following minerals:\nBentonite\nChromite\nKyanite\nSillimanite\nIn India, which are officially designated as major minerals?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"4 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"2, 3 and 4 only","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Mining & Minerals","tags":["Major Minerals","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"The source identifies chromite, kyanite and sillimanite as the major minerals among the listed options.\nBentonite is not included in the source's selected category.\nTherefore, the answer is 2, 3 and 4.","revisionPoints":"Minerals in India are classified under regulatory frameworks.\nMajor minerals are subject to the relevant mining and regulatory provisions.\nChromite → important source of chromium.\nKyanite and sillimanite → important industrial/refractory minerals."},
{"no":93,"text":"With reference to Ocean Mean Temperature (OMT), consider:\nOMT is measured up to the depth of the 26°C isotherm, about 129 metres in the south-western Indian Ocean during January–March.\nOMT collected during January–March can be used to assess whether ensuing monsoon rainfall will be below or above a long-term mean.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Ocean Mean Temperature","Monsoon Forecasting","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"The source identifies only statement 2 as correct.\nOMT is useful as an oceanic indicator in studying the relationship between ocean conditions and the Indian summer monsoon.\nThe numerical/depth specification in statement 1 is not accepted by the source.\nHence, answer B.","revisionPoints":"OMT → Ocean Mean Temperature.\nOcean temperatures influence atmospheric circulation.\nIndian Ocean conditions are important for monsoon variability.\nOceanic indicators can assist in seasonal rainfall assessment."},
{"no":94,"text":"With reference to chemical fertilizers in India, consider:\nAt present, retail prices of chemical fertilizers are market-driven and not administered by the Government.\nAmmonia, an input for urea, is produced from natural gas.\nSulphur, a raw material for phosphoric acid fertilizer, is a by-product of oil refineries.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Fertilizers","Urea","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"The source does not accept statement 1 because fertilizer pricing is not entirely market-driven in the manner stated.\nAmmonia is produced using hydrogen, commonly derived from natural gas in conventional production.\nSulphur can be recovered as a by-product of petroleum refining.\nTherefore, statements 2 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Urea → major nitrogenous fertilizer.\nAmmonia → key input for urea production.\nNatural gas → important feedstock for ammonia production.\nSulphur → important for phosphatic fertilizer production.\nFertilizer pricing/subsidy → important agricultural policy issue."},
{"no":95,"text":"With reference to India's Desert National Park, which statements are correct?\nIt is spread over two districts.\nThere is no human habitation inside the Park.\nIt is one of the natural habitats of the Great Indian Bustard.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Desert National Park","Great Indian Bustard","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Desert National Park extends across two districts of Rajasthan.\nIt is an important habitat for the Great Indian Bustard.\nThe source does not accept the claim that there is no human habitation inside the park.\nHence, statements 1 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Desert National Park → Rajasthan.\nImportant habitat of Great Indian Bustard.\nDesert ecosystem → fragile and highly specialized.\nGreat Indian Bustard → critically endangered bird species.\nHabitat protection is central to its conservation."},
{"no":96,"text":"Siachen Glacier is situated to the:","A":"East of Aksai Chin","B":"East of Leh","C":"North of Gilgit","D":"North of Nubra Valley","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Siachen Glacier","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Siachen Glacier lies in the eastern Karakoram range.\nIt is located in the region north of the Nubra Valley.\nThe glacier is strategically significant because of its location in the high-altitude Karakoram.\nTherefore, option D is correct.","revisionPoints":"Siachen → Karakoram range.\nNubra Valley → lies south of the Siachen Glacier region.\nExtremely high-altitude glacial environment.\nImportant for physical geography as well as strategic geography."},
{"no":97,"text":"With reference to the history of India, consider the following pairs:\nFamous Place\nPresent State\n1. Bhilsa\nMadhya Pradesh\n2. Dwarasamudra\nMaharashtra\n3. Girinagar\nGujarat\n4. Sthanesvara\nUttar Pradesh\nWhich pairs are correctly matched?","A":"1 and 3 only","B":"1 and 4 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"2 and 4 only","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Historical Places","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Bhilsa is identified with present-day Vidisha in Madhya Pradesh.\nGirinagar is associated with Gujarat.\nDwarasamudra and Sthanesvara are incorrectly matched with the States given.\nHence, pairs 1 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Bhilsa → associated with Vidisha, Madhya Pradesh.\nDwarasamudra → associated with the Hoysala region of present-day Karnataka.\nSthanesvara → associated with Thanesar in Haryana.\nAncient place-name questions require mapping historical names to modern locations."},
{"no":98,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n36% of India's districts are classified as “overexploited” or “critical” by the Central Ground Water Authority (CGWA).\nCGWA was formed under the Environment (Protection) Act.\nIndia has the largest area under groundwater irrigation in the world.\nWhich statements are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"2 only","D":"1 and 3 only","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Water Resources","tags":["Groundwater","CGWA","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"The source identifies statements 2 and 3 as correct.\nCGWA is associated with the regulatory framework under the Environment (Protection) Act.\nIndia has a very large area under groundwater irrigation and is a major global user of groundwater.\nStatement 1 is not accepted by the source.","revisionPoints":"CGWA → Central Ground Water Authority.\nGroundwater → major source of irrigation and drinking water in India.\nOver-exploitation → falling groundwater levels and sustainability concerns.\nGroundwater regulation → important environmental governance issue."},
{"no":99,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nJet streams occur in the Northern Hemisphere only.\nOnly some cyclones develop an eye.\nTemperature inside the eye of a cyclone is nearly 10°C less than surroundings.\nWhich statements are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"2 only","D":"1 and 3 only","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Jet Streams","Cyclones","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"Jet streams occur in both hemispheres, so statement 1 is incorrect.\nNot all cyclones develop a clearly defined eye; therefore statement 2 is correct.\nThe temperature inside the eye is not generally 10°C lower than the surroundings; the eye can be relatively warmer aloft because of subsidence.\nTherefore, only statement 2 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Jet streams → narrow bands of strong winds in the upper atmosphere.\nOccur in both Northern and Southern Hemispheres.\nTropical cyclones → may develop an eye at sufficient intensity.\nEye → comparatively calm central region.\nSubsiding air inside the eye can produce warming aloft."},
{"no":100,"text":"Among the following Tiger Reserves, which has the largest area under Critical Tiger Habitat (CTH)?","A":"Corbett","B":"Ranthambore","C":"Nagarjunsagar–Srisailam","D":"Sundarbans","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Tiger Reserves","UPSC","2020"],"expl":"The source identifies Nagarjunsagar–Srisailam Tiger Reserve as having the largest area under Critical Tiger Habitat among the options.\nIts very large forested landscape contributes to its extensive tiger habitat.\nTherefore, option C is correct.","revisionPoints":"Critical Tiger Habitat → core/critical habitat identified for tiger conservation.\nTiger reserves → core and buffer components.\nNagarjunsagar–Srisailam → major tiger landscape of peninsular India.\nTiger conservation involves habitat protection, prey base, corridors and human-wildlife conflict management."},
];
